{
 "title": "The Only Cat Known to Survive Space 🐱🚀 #Shorts",
 "description": "On 18 October 1963, France launched Félicette, a stray tuxedo cat from Paris, on a Véronique rocket from the Sahara in Algeria. She took about 9.5 g and landed by parachute after a flight of under 20 minutes. She is still the only cat known to have flown to space and survived. We also cover how a cat's mid-air twist shaped astronaut research, why cats can't land on their feet in zero gravity, and a bonus fact about purring.\n\nSources:\n- Britannica: Who was the first cat in space?\n- Nautilus: 63 years ago this week, Félicette became the first cat in space\n- BBC Sky at Night Magazine: Félicette, the first cat in space\n- Space.com: On this day in space, Oct. 18\n- CBC: Statue of Félicette unveiled\n- ScienceAlert: Scientists put cats in microgravity\n- Nagoya J. Med. Sci. (1995): Disorientation of animals in microgravity\n- Scientific American: Why do cats purr?\n- Science World: The secret power of a cat's purr\n\nPart of the Space Is Weird series. Visuals and voice are AI-assisted.",
